WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:06.701
Next question today, David, as far as the
2020 Democratic primary campaign goes, are

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:13.220
Democrats better off, going hard, left or
appealing to moderates for the purpose of

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.000
ultimately defeating Trump in 2020?

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.039
Excellent question.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:24.609
And the answer to me is so clear that I don't
want to equivocate in any way appealing to

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:31.509
moderates has never been a good strategy and
it has never demonstrably worked at the national

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:32.509
level.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:38.050
Now we can separate this out from particular
districts or even state Senate seats, a Senate

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.020
seats, which are the voting public isn't entire
state is what I'm trying to say.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:48.370
It's the wrong approach to even be thinking
in these terms though, and that's what really

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:49.580
scares me.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.510
Democrats shouldn't calculate should we go
hard left or should we deliberately go to

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:03.260
the middle in order to defeat Donald Trump
in 2020 or for for whatever particular reason,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.950
Democrats and people who are voting in democratic
primaries and people who are voting in general

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.740
should vote for who they want to vote for
on the basis of how those individuals lineup

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.300
with their beliefs.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.020
And it's perfectly reason to think about how
those individuals would fare in a national

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:26.780
campaign who can avoid mistakes, who can handle
gaffes, who can deal with attack opposition

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.090
research.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:32.730
Those are the questions that need to be asked,
not let's calculate where on the political

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.730
spectrum.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:40.340
We want to pick someone with the idea of bringing
in as many moderates and center rightists

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.320
as possible, who might be disaffected by Donald
Trump or whoever is the republican that we

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.800
are running against.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.729
Pick the person that you believe is best suited
for a national campaign and whose politics

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:53.190
aligned with yours.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.510
Make that person the nominee and then they
can run against Trump.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:01.100
And the question is not to calculate who's
going to do better against Trump based on

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:08.560
are they center or center left or far further
left the, the question is, uh, who is it that

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.860
actually represents the views of Americans
and what we know from every single poll that

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.910
fairly asks, uh, Americans about their political
positions.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:24.230
We know that Americans are weld to the left
of the elected officials that are representing

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.230
us.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.830
When you fairly go to people and say, do you
think that the government has a basic duty

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:36.690
to provide a, a sort of starter, at least
a starter level of care to everybody regardless

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.390
of ability to pay?

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:43.380
Overwhelmingly two thirds to 80% of Americans
depending on, depending on the poll, say yeah,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.709
I think that's something that should happen.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:49.080
Now when you politicize it in the way that
it gets politicized, do you want the government

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.920
taking over healthcare and a uh, a socialist
and manner?

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.849
Of course people start to say, oh no, I don't
want that.

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:03.520
But on the issues when asked fairly income
inequality, health care, net neutrality, whatever,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.530
um, the country has moved to the left and
our politicians have not.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:13.300
So the question is not let's calculate left
center, Middle Center, left or whatever.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.190
Let's go through the primary process supporting
the people whose views reflect our views.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:22.750
Let's see who avoid mistakes and who can deal
with the gaffs and the stumbles and people

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.720
will start to emerge as leaders and we will
see whether they are going to be able to handle

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.360
the attacks that no doubt they will be presented
with if they are the Democratic nominee.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:38.709
So the calculation that we need to make is
not one of what will moderates and disaffected

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:40.280
Republicans like.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.730
Now, let's put aside for a second that I believe
this is the wrong question altogether.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:49.060
The idea of running to the center has never
worked.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.060
Okay?

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:57.330
There's this idea that if we as Democrats,
progressives left this, whatever, pick someone

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.629
that is closer to the center, we will pick
up centrists and we'll win because someone

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:06.900
will run numbers and say, you know, if we
get just a three additional percentage points

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.780
of the independent vote, uh, Republicans can't
win or whatever at the national level, this

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:12.870
just haven't worked.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:13.870
Okay.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:20.180
I would argue that save for Conservative districts,
Democrats running to the center is a disastrous

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.180
strategy.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.130
There are house elect elections, okay.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:29.560
Where we make the calculation and we say,
okay, what kind of Democrat can win in Virginia?

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.110
Uh, that's a different type of Democrat and
the Democrat that can win in coastal California

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.030
or in Massachusetts for example.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.590
Uh, but the primary process already sorts
that out nationally.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.910
This ultimately comes down to personality,
communicating ideas.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.979
Well, staving off attacks from the other side
and avoiding scandal.

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:55.919
So we might as well just put somebody in whose
politics match those of the leftist voters

00:04:55.919 --> 00:04:59.820
that are making the decision here and of Americans
more broadly.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.240
And those have moved to the left, as I mentioned,
on just about every issue, taxing the rich

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.330
to pay for things.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:09.850
I mentioned healthcare already, seriously
dealing with climate change, campaign finance,

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:13.030
staying out of places that aren't our business
around the world.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.900
Pick the person that appeals to you on policy
and likability, which we know for the Democratic

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:23.410
primary voters is someone that is further
left of center than many of the people that

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:27.870
we often hear suggested as we've got to run
to the middle to bring people from the other

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:28.870
side over.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:29.870
It hasn't worked.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:30.900
It's not a defensible strategy.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:31.510
Let's stop pretending that it is.

